 edge over Graham's modest war chest", "Recent polls show a surprisingly tight race, though most come from partisan sponsors", "Markets and most expert ratings still favor Graham decisively"], "rationale": "The state's Republican lean and incumbent advantage point to a Graham win, but Andrews has outraised the incumbent by a wide margin and the latest polling averages sit around even to Graham plus five, prompting raters like Cook to shift the race toward Democrats. Markets still price Andrews near 15 percent, so the forecast stays at a modest Republican edge rather than Likely R.", "watch": "Any post-Labor Day independent polls confirming the tight averages, or major outside spending against Graham, would move this toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race pairs interim appointee Darline Graham, a Republican running for a full term, against Democrat Annie Andrews, a well-funded challenger in a state that leans about eight points Republican. Graham has raised little herself but benefits from roughly a million dollars in outside support, while Andrews has built a large campaign bankroll and drawn no significant outside opposition so far.\n\nPolling has been unusually tight, with averages placing Graham ahead by only a few points, though much of it comes from partisan-sponsored surveys and the sample of credible polls is thin. Expert raters mostly call the race Likely Republican, with several moving it toward Lean Republican in late September, while prediction markets still give Andrews only about a 15 percent chance. The balance of evidence favors Graham, but the margin looks narrower than South Carolina's typical Senate contests.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}]}